nly features are a small louvered door on the south wall, a larger arched door on the north wall, and a stair which spirals downward. The large door is open but the small door is closed. Weird Passageway You are in a disturbing hallway; the lines of the walls are disorienting in a strange, non-Euclidean sort of way. The passageway curves sharply to the left here, ending at an archway to the west. To the south, the passage ends at a large arched door which is open. Weird Passageway You are in a disturbing hallway; the lines of the walls are disorienting in a strange, non-Euclidean sort of way. The passageway curves sharply to the left here, ending at an archway to the west. To the south, the passage ends at a large arched door which is open. Pyramid Room This room, though rather wide at floor level, narrows as it rises like the interior of a pyramid or of an enormous beehive. The only exit is east. You can see a cup here. It seems that the cup contains magic potion. [Your score has just gone up by 7.] Pyramid Room This room, though rather wide at floor level, narrows as it rises like the interior of a pyramid or of an enormous beehive. The only exit is east. You can see a cup here. It seems that the cup contains magic potion. You're already juggling as many items as you could possibly carry. Weird Passageway Weird Passageway You are in a disturbing hallway; the lines of the walls are disorienting in a strange, non-Euclidean sort of way. The passageway curves sharply to the left here, ending at an archway to the west. To the south, the passage ends at a large arched door which is open. Jester's Quarters The jester appears. The jester watches you with twinkling eyes. Jester's Quarters This is a small, spare room. The only features are a small louvered door on the south wall, a larger arched door on the north wall, and a stair which spirals downward. The large door is open but the small door is closed. The jester is nearby, watching you with great amusement. Servants' Quarters Servants' Quarters These are the spartan rooms where the servants lived (if you can actually call this "living"). A passage leads west and a narrow stair spirals upward. Guest Rooms Guest Rooms This is where visitors to the castle would stay. Many stayed for decades. Passages lead east and west. East Wing East Wing This wide corridor runs the length of the east wing of the castle. The corridor ends at a large door to the west. There are openings to the north, south and east. East Hall You can see an iron key here. East Hall The castle's east wing lies through a massive door to the east, which is open. The only other exit from the hall is northwest. You can see an iron key here. Courtyard Courtyard This open area is paved with marble and surrounded by imposing stone walls. Dimwit would occasionally order carnivals to be set up in this court, on totally cloudless days. At the distant edges of the courtyard, you can see exits to the north, west, and southeast. Banquet Hall You can see a cauldron here. Banquet Hall Many royal feasts have been held in this hall, which could easily hold ten thousand guests. Legends say that Dimwit's more excessive banquets would require the combined farm outputs of three provinces. The primary exits are to the west and south; smaller openings lead east and northeast. You can see a cauldron here. With a puff of magically charged smoke, the lance vanishes amidst the vapors. The cauldron's level of activity seems to increase. [Your score has just gone up by 5.] With a puff of magically charged smoke, the manuscript vanishes amidst the vapors. The cauldron's level of activity seems to increase; it is now bubbling very actively. [Your score has just gone up by 5.] You are carrying a wand, a dusty slate, a package, a zorkmid coin, a steel key, a worm, an unopened walnut, a cloak and a candle (providing light). It seems that the package contains a ceramic pigeon and a ceramic perch. You are wearing a pair of goggles, a seaman's cap and a glove. Courtyard Courtyard This open area is paved with marble and surrounded by imposing stone walls. Dimwit would occasionally order carnivals to be set up in this court, on totally cloudless days. At the distant edges of the courtyard, you can see exits to the north, west, and southeast. East Hall You can see an iron key here. East Hall The castle's east wing lies through a massive door to the east, which is open. The only other exit from the hall is northwest. You can see an iron key here. East Wing East Wing This wide corridor runs the length of the east wing of the castle. The corridor ends at a large door to the west. There are openings to the north, south and east. Guest Rooms Guest Rooms This is where visitors to the castle would stay. Many stayed for decades. Passages lead east and west. Servants' Quarters Servants' Quarters These are the spartan rooms where the servants lived (if you can actually call this "living"). A passage leads west and a narrow stair spirals upward. Jester's Quarters Jester's Quarters This is a small, spare room. The only features are a small louvered door on the south wall, a larger arched door on the north wall, and a stair which spirals downward. The large door is open but the small door is closed. Weird Passageway Weird Passageway You are in a disturbing hallway; the lines of the walls are disorienting in a strange, non-Euclidean sort of way. The passageway curves sharply to the left here, ending at an archway to the west. To the south, the passage ends at a large arched door which is open. Pyramid Room You can see a cup here. It seems that the cup contains magic potion. Pyramid Room This room, though rather wide at floor level, narrows as it rises like the interior of a pyramid or of an enormous beehive. The only exit is east. You can see a cup here. It seems that the cup contains magic potion. You pick up the cup. The jester appears. The jester watches you with twinkling eyes. Weird Passageway Weird Passageway You are in a disturbing hallway; the lines of the walls are disorienting in a strange, non-Euclidean sort of way. The passageway curves sharply to the left here, ending at an archway to the west. To the south, the passage ends at a large arched door which is open. Jester's Quarters Jester's Quarters This is a small, spare room. The only features are a small louvered door on the south wall, a larger arched door on the north wall, and a stair which spirals downward. The large door is open but the small door is closed. Servants' Quarters Servants' Quarters These are the spartan rooms where the servants lived (if you can actually call this "living"). A passage leads west and a narrow stair spirals upward. Guest Rooms Guest Rooms This is where visitors to the castle would stay. Many stayed for decades. Passages lead east and west. East Wing East Wing This wide corridor runs the length of the east wing of the castle. The corridor ends at a large door to the west. There are openings to the north, south and east. East Hall You can see an iron key here. East Hall The castle's east wing lies through a massive door to the east, which is open. The only other exit from the hall is northwest. You can see an iron key here. Courtyard Courtyard This open area is paved with marble and surrounded by imposing stone walls. Dimwit would occasionally order carnivals to be set up in this court, on totally cloudless days. At the distant edges of the courtyard, you can see exits to the north, west, and southeast. Great Hall Great Hall This is the huge central chamber of Dimwit's castle. The ceiling was lowered at some point in the past, which helped reduce the frequency of storm clouds forming in the upper regions of the hall. Arched openings lead off in the four cardinal directions. A wide stair leads up to the balcony, and an equally wide but ominously dark stair leads downward. Formal Garden Dimwit designed this garden to match a fairy tale he enjoyed as a child, and subsequent kings added their own touches, creating a hugely confusing maze of flowers and shrubbery and statuary and trees and fountains and pools and bridges and gazebos. Now somewhat overgrown, the garden is seemingly endless, but you recall that the primary exits lie to the north, east, and southwest. You spot a flash of pink amongst the flora. It's a flamingo! Formal Garden Dimwit designed this garden to match a fairy tale he enjoyed as a child, and subsequent kings added their own touches, creating a hugely confusing maze of flowers and shrubbery and statuary and trees and fountains and pools and bridges and gazebos. Now somewhat overgrown, the garden is seemingly endless, but you recall that the primary exits lie to the north, east, and southwest. You spot a flash of pink amongst the flora. It's a flamingo! Parlor This is where many of Dimwit's guests would come and sit after dinner, for wine, conversation, and any other whim that might pop into Dimwit's head. There are doorways to the east, west and south. You can see a fish tank here. It seems that the fish tank contains a clump of spenseweed, a starfish and a lobster. Parlor This is where many of Dimwit's guests would come and sit after dinner, for wine, conversation, and any other whim that might pop into Dimwit's head. There are doorways to the east, west and south. You can see a fish tank here. It seems that the fish tank contains a clump of spenseweed, a starfish and a lobster. The lobster stops moving. Frozen as it is, with pincers outstretched, it looks like nothing less than a large nutcracker. In fact, it IS a nutcracker! Parlor This is where many of Dimwit's guests would come and sit after dinner, for wine, conversation, and any other whim that might pop into Dimwit's head. There are doorways to the east, west and south. You can see a fish tank here. It seems that the fish tank contains a clump of spenseweed, a starfish and a nutcracker. You take the nutcracker from the fish tank. Formal Garden You spot a flash of pink amongst the fl