s with interest. "Crack!" Opening the walnut shell reveals a walnut. "True, no one has seen this 'ere me -- but thousands may see it in years to be!" "I'm very impressed; you passed my test! When you exit from the West Wing, I'll no longer be molesting." The jester gives a cheerful wave of farewell and vanishes, saying, "See you in the funny papers!" A breeze begins picking up. [Your score has just gone up by 20.] Youch! The nutcracker just pinched you! As you drop it, you realize that it has turned back into a lobster. West Wing This wing exhibits more recent styling: smoothly dressed stone inlaid with marble patterns. Through the open oak door to the east lie the primary halls of the castle. Other exits lead north, west, and south. A faint smell of fudge hangs in the air. You can see a lobster here. The wind blows a colorful piece of newspaper into view. It's a funny paper! You pick up the funny paper. It's a comic strip entitled "Me and the Jester." In the strip, a peasant adventurer is bumbling around a mighty castle, alternately helped and hindered by a playful jester. In one panel, the Jester suggests that the adventurer look under the newest slab in the Crypt. Dropped. Dropped. Peg Room You're in a nondescript room with an exit to the south. In the center of the room is a pegboard. Hanging on the wall are a set of instructions. Peg Room You're in a nondescript room with an exit to the south. In the center of the room is a pegboard. Hanging on the wall are a set of instructions. Type the letter corresponding to the peg you'd like to move, or use your mouse/joystick to click on it. Type X to exit Peggleboz, Y to display your moves so far, Z to reset the board (or you can use your mouse/joystick to click on the appropriate spot). You are moving the peg at letter Q. Select the letter for the destination point of that peg, or hit Q again to "unselect" that peg. Type the letter corresponding to the peg you'd like to move, or use your mouse/joystick to click on it. 